  "hypothesis_one_liner": "Higher household-level 1989 net saving flow (our ordinal proxy for *lower* time preference) is associated with lower 1989 BMI in this NLSY79 cross-section: a one-unit shift toward 'PUT MORE IN' on the 1989 savings-action scale is linked to a -0.30 kg/m^2 lower BMI in 1989 (95% CI [-0.45,-0.14], p=1.85e-04, n=6,074; standardized beta=-0.056), and reduces the odds of obesity (BMI>=30; prevalence 12.3%) by ~12.4% per unit shift (OR=0.88, 95% CI [0.82,0.94]). The pattern persists in 1992 BMI (-0.36 kg/m^2, p=2.23e-04, n=5,062) and is sharper in the unmarried-with-parents sub-sample (-0.45 kg/m^2, p=0.032, n=487), where the household-level savings proxy is not diluted by a spouse. Same-sign, smaller-magnitude association in the BMI_1981 negative control (-0.20 kg/m^2, p=0.046); that sample uses height-1981 captured during ongoing adolescent growth so its interpretation is attenuated. We treat this as *associational* evidence on household-level savings flow (not individual-level discount rate) due to (i) no experimental time-preference measure, (ii) spouse dilution, and (iii) three unavoidable OVB sources: no physical-activity / diet / smoking controls in this subset, no pregnancy indicator, and attrition that retains +34-ASVAB-point higher-ability respondents. The 2.2046 coefficient ratio under 'kg' vs 'lb' weight assumption exactly matches the kg/lb conversion (2.2046), confirming that the field documentation is wrong and the weight columns are in pounds.",
